{
 "version": 1,
 "id": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000302",
 "title": "Lagrange Points",
 "created_at": "2026-03-26T00:00:00Z",
 "updated_at": "2026-07-12T00:00:00Z",
 "shared_cargo_toml": "[profile.release]\nopt-level = 1\nlto = false\ncodegen-units = 16",
 "shared_source": null,
 "cells": [
  {
   "id": "cell_0",
   "order": 0,
   "label": "Introduction",
   "cell_type": "Markdown",
   "source": "## Introduction\n\nIn celestial mechanics, the **Lagrange points** are five positions in a two-body gravitational system where a small object can remain stationary relative to the two large bodies.\n\n## The Circular Restricted Three-Body Problem (CR3BP)\n\nWe work in a **co-rotating frame** that spins with the two primary bodies ($M_1$, $M_2$). In this frame, both primaries are stationary and the dynamics of a test particle are governed by an effective potential that combines gravity and centrifugal effects:\n\n$$\\Phi(x, y) = -\\frac{1-\\mu}{r_1} - \\frac{\\mu}{r_2} - \\frac{1}{2}(x^2 + y^2)$$\n\nwhere $\\mu = M_2 / (M_1 + M_2)$ is the mass ratio, and $r_1$, $r_2$ are distances to each primary. The full setup, including the one force this potential *cannot* capture, gets a proper treatment below the simulation.\n\n## The Five Points\n\n| Point | Location | Stability |\n|-------|----------|-----------|\n| **L1** | Between the two bodies | Unstable (saddle) |\n| **L2** | Beyond the smaller body | Unstable (saddle) |\n| **L3** | Beyond the larger body | Unstable (saddle) |\n| **L4** | 60\u00b0 ahead in orbit | Stable (for $\\mu < 0.0385$) |\n| **L5** | 60\u00b0 behind in orbit | Stable (for $\\mu < 0.0385$) |\n\nL4 and L5 form equilateral triangles with the two primaries; these are the famous **Trojan points** where Jupiter's Trojan asteroids collect.\n\n## L2 Orbiter\n\nThe white diamond shows a spacecraft in a **Lissajous orbit** around L2, the same type of orbit JWST uses. L2 is unstable, so the spacecraft doesn't sit *on* the point; it orbits *around* it in a quasi-periodic pattern. Real missions require periodic station-keeping burns to prevent escape.\n\nUse the **Frame** slider to switch between the co-rotating view (where L-points are fixed) and the inertial view (where everything orbits)!",
   "cargo_toml": null
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_controls",
   "order": 1,
   "label": "Controls",
   "cell_type": "Code",
   "source": "(\n    ui::slider(\"speed\", 1.0, 20.0)\n        .step(1.0)\n        .label(\"Simulation Speed\")\n        .default_value(5.0),\n    ui::dropdown(&[\"Co-rotating\", \"Inertial\"])\n        .label(\"Reference Frame\")\n        .name(\"frame\")\n        .default_value(\"Co-rotating\"),\n)",
   "cargo_toml": null,
   "collapsed": true
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_sim",
   "order": 2,
   "label": "Live Simulation",
   "cell_type": "Code",
   "source": "// Lagrange Points: live simulation with frame selection.\n//\n// Shows two primaries, all five L-points, test particles, and an L2\n// Lissajous orbiter. Toggle between co-rotating and inertial frames.\n\nconst WIDTH: u32 = 800;\nconst HEIGHT: u32 = 800;\nconst MU: f64 = 0.012;\nconst DT: f64 = 0.0005;\nconst MAX_TRAIL: usize = 600;\nconst SCALE: f64 = 180.0;\nconst CX: f64 = 400.0;\nconst CY: f64 = 400.0;\nconst PI: f64 = std::f64::consts::PI;\n\nfn to_px(x: f64, y: f64) -> (i32, i32) {\n    ((CX + x * SCALE) as i32, (CY - y * SCALE) as i32)\n}\n\n// Rotate from co-rotating frame to inertial frame.\nfn rotate(x: f64, y: f64, theta: f64) -> (f64, f64) {\n    let c = theta.cos();\n    let s = theta.sin();\n    (x * c - y * s, x * s + y * c)\n}\n\n// \u2500\u2500 CR3BP equations of motion in the rotating frame \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nfn derivs(s: &[f64; 4], mu: f64) -> [f64; 4] {\n    let (x, y, vx, vy) = (s[0], s[1], s[2], s[3]);\n    let x1 = -mu;\n    let x2 = 1.0 - mu;\n    let r1 = ((x - x1) * (x - x1) + y * y).sqrt();\n    let r2 = ((x - x2) * (x - x2) + y * y).sqrt();\n    let r1_3 = r1 * r1 * r1;\n    let r2_3 = r2 * r2 * r2;\n    let ax = x + 2.0 * vy - (1.0 - mu) * (x - x1) / r1_3 - mu * (x - x2) / r2_3;\n    let ay = y - 2.0 * vx - (1.0 - mu) * y / r1_3 - mu * y / r2_3;\n    [vx, vy, ax, ay]\n}\n\nfn rk4(s: &mut [f64; 4], dt: f64, mu: f64) {\n    let k1 = derivs(s, mu);\n    let mut s2 = *s;\n    for i in 0..4 { s2[i] = s[i] + 0.5 * dt * k1[i]; }\n    let k2 = derivs(&s2, mu);\n    let mut s3 = *s;\n    for i in 0..4 { s3[i] = s[i] + 0.5 * dt * k2[i]; }\n    let k3 = derivs(&s3, mu);\n    let mut s4 = *s;\n    for i in 0..4 { s4[i] = s[i] + dt * k3[i]; }\n    let k4 = derivs(&s4, mu);\n    for i in 0..4 {\n        s[i] += dt / 6.0 * (k1[i] + 2.0 * k2[i] + 2.0 * k3[i] + k4[i]);\n    }\n}\n\n// \u2500\u2500 Newton solver for collinear L-points \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nfn equil_force(x: f64, mu: f64) -> f64 {\n    let x1 = -mu;\n    let x2 = 1.0 - mu;\n    let r1 = (x - x1).abs();\n    let r2 = (x - x2).abs();\n    x - (1.0 - mu) * (x - x1) / (r1 * r1 * r1) - mu * (x - x2) / (r2 * r2 * r2)\n}\n\n// Bisection solver: guaranteed to find root in [lo, hi].\nfn solve_bisect(mut lo: f64, mut hi: f64, mu: f64) -> f64 {\n    let f_lo = equil_force(lo, mu);\n    for _ in 0..100 {\n        let mid = 0.5 * (lo + hi);\n        let f_mid = equil_force(mid, mu);\n        if f_mid * f_lo < 0.0 { hi = mid; } else { lo = mid; }\n    }\n    0.5 * (lo + hi)\n}\n\n// \u2500\u2500 Drawing helpers \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nfn draw_circle(c: &mut Canvas, cx: i32, cy: i32, r: i32, color: (u8, u8, u8)) {\n    for dy in -r..=r {\n        for dx in -r..=r {\n            if dx * dx + dy * dy <= r * r {\n                let px = cx + dx;\n                let py = cy + dy;\n                if px >= 0 && py >= 0 && (px as u32) < WIDTH && (py as u32) < HEIGHT {\n                    c.set_pixel(px as u32, py as u32, color);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn draw_diamond(c: &mut Canvas, cx: i32, cy: i32, r: i32, color: (u8, u8, u8)) {\n    for dy in -r..=r {\n        for dx in -r..=r {\n            if dx.abs() + dy.abs() <= r {\n                let px = cx + dx;\n                let py = cy + dy;\n                if px >= 0 && py >= 0 && (px as u32) < WIDTH && (py as u32) < HEIGHT {\n                    c.set_pixel(px as u32, py as u32, color);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn draw_cross(c: &mut Canvas, cx: i32, cy: i32, size: i32, color: (u8, u8, u8)) {\n    for d in -size..=size {\n        if cx + d >= 0 && ((cx + d) as u32) < WIDTH && cy >= 0 && (cy as u32) < HEIGHT {\n            c.set_pixel((cx + d) as u32, cy as u32, color);\n        }\n        if cx >= 0 && (cx as u32) < WIDTH && cy + d >= 0 && ((cy + d) as u32) < HEIGHT {\n            c.set_pixel(cx as u32, (cy + d) as u32, color);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn draw_label(c: &mut Canvas, text: &str, x: i32, y: i32, color: (u8, u8, u8)) {\n    let glyphs: &[(&str, [u8; 5])] = &[\n        (\"L\", [0b100, 0b100, 0b100, 0b100, 0b111]),\n        (\"1\", [0b010, 0b110, 0b010, 0b010, 0b111]),\n        (\"2\", [0b111, 0b001, 0b111, 0b100, 0b111]),\n        (\"3\", [0b111, 0b001, 0b111, 0b001, 0b111]),\n        (\"4\", [0b101, 0b101, 0b111, 0b001, 0b001]),\n        (\"5\", [0b111, 0b100, 0b111, 0b001, 0b111]),\n        (\"M\", [0b101, 0b111, 0b111, 0b101, 0b101]),\n    ];\n    let mut ox = 0_i32;\n    for ch in text.chars() {\n        let ch_str = ch.to_string();\n        if let Some((_, rows)) = glyphs.iter().find(|(g, _)| *g == ch_str) {\n            for (row, bits) in rows.iter().enumerate() {\n                for col in 0..3_i32 {\n                    if bits & (1 << (2 - col)) != 0 {\n                        let px = x + ox + col;\n                        let py = y + row as i32;\n                        if px >= 0 && py >= 0 && (px as u32) < WIDTH && (py as u32) < HEIGHT {\n                            c.set_pixel(px as u32, py as u32, color);\n                        }\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        ox += 4;\n    }\n}\n\nfn set_thick(c: &mut Canvas, px: i32, py: i32, color: (u8, u8, u8)) {\n    for dy in 0..2_i32 {\n        for dx in 0..2_i32 {\n            let x = px + dx;\n            let y = py + dy;\n            if x >= 0 && y >= 0 && (x as u32) < WIDTH && (y as u32) < HEIGHT {\n                c.set_pixel(x as u32, y as u32, color);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// \u2500\u2500 Simulation state \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nstruct Particle {\n    state: [f64; 4],\n    trail: Vec<(f64, f64, f64)>, // (x, y, time) in rotating frame\n    color: (u8, u8, u8),\n}\n\nstruct LagrangeSim {\n    particles: Vec<Particle>,\n    orbiter: Particle,\n    lpoints: [(f64, f64); 5],\n    sim_time: f64,\n    orbiter_init: [f64; 4],\n    l2_pos: (f64, f64),\n}\n\nimpl Simulation for LagrangeSim {\n    fn init() -> Self {\n        let mu = MU;\n        let x1 = -mu;\n        let x2 = 1.0 - mu;\n\n        // Bisection brackets for each collinear L-point.\n        let l1 = (solve_bisect(x1 + 0.01, x2 - 0.01, mu), 0.0);\n        let l2 = (solve_bisect(x2 + 0.01, x2 + 0.5, mu), 0.0);\n        let l3 = (solve_bisect(-2.0, x1 - 0.01, mu), 0.0);\n        let l4 = (0.5 - mu, (3.0_f64).sqrt() / 2.0);\n        let l5 = (0.5 - mu, -(3.0_f64).sqrt() / 2.0);\n        let lpoints = [l1, l2, l3, l4, l5];\n\n        let configs: [(usize, f64, f64, f64, f64, (u8, u8, u8)); 5] = [\n            (0, 0.004, 0.0,   0.0,  0.006, (255, 80, 80)),\n            (1, 0.004, 0.0,   0.0, -0.006, (255, 160, 50)),\n            (2, 0.004, 0.0,   0.0,  0.004, (200, 80, 255)),\n            (3, 0.003, 0.003, 0.0,  0.0,   (80, 255, 120)),\n            (4, 0.003,-0.003, 0.0,  0.0,   (50, 200, 255)),\n        ];\n\n        let particles: Vec<Particle> = configs.iter().map(|&(i, dx, dy, dvx, dvy, color)| {\n            Particle {\n                state: [lpoints[i].0 + dx, lpoints[i].1 + dy, dvx, dvy],\n                trail: Vec::with_capacity(MAX_TRAIL),\n                color,\n            }\n        }).collect();\n\n        // L2 Lissajous orbiter: small perturbation with station-keeping.\n        // Real spacecraft (JWST) perform periodic burns to stay near L2.\n        // Initial vy tuned to approximate the periodic orbit frequency.\n        let orbiter_init: [f64; 4] = [l2.0 + 0.005, 0.0, 0.0, -0.022];\n        let orbiter = Particle {\n            state: orbiter_init,\n            trail: Vec::with_capacity(MAX_TRAIL),\n            color: (255, 255, 255),\n        };\n\n        Self { particles, orbiter, lpoints, sim_time: 0.0,\n               orbiter_init, l2_pos: l2 }\n    }\n\n    fn tick(&mut self) -> Canvas {\n        let mu = MU;\n        let speed = sim::read::<f64>(\"speed\").unwrap_or(5.0) as usize;\n        let inertial = sim::read::<f64>(\"frame\").unwrap_or(0.0) > 0.5;\n\n        let sub_steps = speed * 20;\n        let dt_total = DT * sub_steps as f64;\n\n        // Integrate all particles\n        for p in &mut self.particles {\n            for _ in 0..sub_steps {\n                rk4(&mut p.state, DT, mu);\n            }\n            p.trail.push((p.state[0], p.state[1], self.sim_time + dt_total));\n            if p.trail.len() > MAX_TRAIL {\n                p.trail.remove(0);\n            }\n        }\n\n        // Integrate orbiter with station-keeping delta-v burns.\n        // When it drifts too far from L2, cancel radial velocity and\n        // add an inward kick, like JWST's thruster corrections.\n        for _ in 0..sub_steps {\n            rk4(&mut self.orbiter.state, DT, mu);\n            if self.orbiter.state[0].is_nan() {\n                self.orbiter.state = self.orbiter_init;\n                self.orbiter.trail.clear();\n                break;\n            }\n            let dx = self.orbiter.state[0] - self.l2_pos.0;\n            let dy = self.orbiter.state[1] - self.l2_pos.1;\n            let dist = (dx * dx + dy * dy).sqrt();\n            if dist > 0.035 {\n                let ux = dx / dist;\n                let uy = dy / dist;\n                // Radial velocity (positive = escaping)\n                let vr = self.orbiter.state[2] * ux\n                       + self.orbiter.state[3] * uy;\n                // Cancel outward drift + inward kick\n                let kick = 0.02;\n                self.orbiter.state[2] -= (vr + kick) * ux;\n                self.orbiter.state[3] -= (vr + kick) * uy;\n            }\n        }\n        self.orbiter.trail.push((self.orbiter.state[0], self.orbiter.state[1],\n                                 self.sim_time + dt_total));\n        if self.orbiter.trail.len() > MAX_TRAIL {\n            self.orbiter.trail.remove(0);\n        }\n\n        self.sim_time += dt_total;\n\n        // \u2500\u2500 Render \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n        let mut canvas = Canvas::new(WIDTH, HEIGHT);\n        canvas.fill((8, 8, 18));\n\n        // Helper: transform (x_rot, y_rot) at time t to display coords.\n        let xf = |x: f64, y: f64, t: f64| -> (f64, f64) {\n            if inertial { rotate(x, y, t) } else { (x, y) }\n        };\n\n        // Draw grid (always axis-aligned in display frame)\n        for i in 0..WIDTH {\n            for j in 0..HEIGHT {\n                let wx = (i as f64 - CX) / SCALE;\n                let wy = (CY - j as f64) / SCALE;\n                let gx = (wx * 2.0).fract().abs();\n                let gy = (wy * 2.0).fract().abs();\n                if gx < 0.008 || gy < 0.008 {\n                    canvas.set_pixel(i, j, (20, 20, 35));\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        // Draw axes\n        for px in 0..WIDTH {\n            canvas.set_pixel(px, CY as u32, (30, 30, 50));\n        }\n        for py in 0..HEIGHT {\n            canvas.set_pixel(CX as u32, py, (30, 30, 50));\n        }\n\n        // Draw reference circle (unit distance from barycenter)\n        for k in 0..720 {\n            let angle = 2.0 * PI * k as f64 / 720.0;\n            let (px, py) = to_px(angle.cos(), angle.sin());\n            if px >= 0 && py >= 0 && (px as u32) < WIDTH && (py as u32) < HEIGHT {\n                canvas.set_pixel(px as u32, py as u32, (25, 25, 45));\n            }\n        }\n\n        // In inertial frame, draw the orbit paths of the primaries\n        if inertial {\n            let r1 = MU; // M1 distance from barycenter\n            let r2 = 1.0 - MU; // M2 distance from barycenter\n            for k in 0..720 {\n                let angle = 2.0 * PI * k as f64 / 720.0;\n                let (px1, py1) = to_px(r1 * angle.cos(), r1 * angle.sin());\n                if px1 >= 0 && py1 >= 0 && (px1 as u32) < WIDTH && (py1 as u32) < HEIGHT {\n                    canvas.set_pixel(px1 as u32, py1 as u32, (40, 35, 20));\n                }\n                let (px2, py2) = to_px(r2 * angle.cos(), r2 * angle.sin());\n                if px2 >= 0 && py2 >= 0 && (px2 as u32) < WIDTH && (py2 as u32) < HEIGHT {\n                    canvas.set_pixel(px2 as u32, py2 as u32, (20, 30, 45));\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        // Draw trails (transform each point from rotating to display frame)\n        for p in self.particles.iter().chain(std::iter::once(&self.orbiter)) {\n            let n = p.trail.len();\n            for (i, &(tx, ty, tt)) in p.trail.iter().enumerate() {\n                let t_frac = i as f64 / n as f64;\n                let (r, g, b) = p.color;\n                let fade = (t_frac * t_frac * 255.0) as u8;\n                let cr = ((r as f64) * fade as f64 / 255.0) as u8;\n                let cg = ((g as f64) * fade as f64 / 255.0) as u8;\n                let cb = ((b as f64) * fade as f64 / 255.0) as u8;\n                let (dx, dy) = xf(tx, ty, tt);\n                let (px, py) = to_px(dx, dy);\n                set_thick(&mut canvas, px, py, (cr, cg, cb));\n            }\n        }\n\n        // Draw Lagrange point markers\n        let lp_colors: [(u8, u8, u8); 5] = [\n            (255, 80, 80), (255, 160, 50), (200, 80, 255),\n            (80, 255, 120), (50, 200, 255),\n        ];\n        let lp_labels = [\"L1\", \"L2\", \"L3\", \"L4\", \"L5\"];\n        for (i, &(lx, ly)) in self.lpoints.iter().enumerate() {\n            let (dx, dy) = xf(lx, ly, self.sim_time);\n            let (px, py) = to_px(dx, dy);\n            draw_cross(&mut canvas, px, py, 4, lp_colors[i]);\n            draw_label(&mut canvas, lp_labels[i], px + 7, py - 6, lp_colors[i]);\n        }\n\n        // Draw test particle current positions\n        for p in &self.particles {\n            let (dx, dy) = xf(p.state[0], p.state[1], self.sim_time);\n            let (px, py) = to_px(dx, dy);\n            draw_circle(&mut canvas, px, py, 3, p.color);\n        }\n\n        // Draw L2 orbiter as a diamond\n        let (ox, oy) = xf(self.orbiter.state[0], self.orbiter.state[1], self.sim_time);\n        let (opx, opy) = to_px(ox, oy);\n        draw_diamond(&mut canvas, opx, opy, 5, (255, 255, 255));\n\n        // Draw primaries\n        let (m1dx, m1dy) = xf(-mu, 0.0, self.sim_time);\n        let (m1x, m1y) = to_px(m1dx, m1dy);\n        draw_circle(&mut canvas, m1x, m1y, 8, (255, 220, 50));\n        draw_label(&mut canvas, \"M1\", m1x + 10, m1y - 3, (255, 220, 50));\n\n        let (m2dx, m2dy) = xf(1.0 - mu, 0.0, self.sim_time);\n        let (m2x, m2y) = to_px(m2dx, m2dy);\n        draw_circle(&mut canvas, m2x, m2y, 5, (100, 180, 255));\n        draw_label(&mut canvas, \"M2\", m2x + 8, m2y - 3, (100, 180, 255));\n\n        // Publish state for dashboard\n        sim::emit(\"time\", &self.sim_time);\n        sim::emit(\"inertial\", &inertial);\n\n        canvas\n    }\n\n    fn fps() -> u32 {\n        30\n    }\n}",
   "cargo_toml": null,
   "collapsed": true
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_dashboard",
   "order": 3,
   "label": "Status Dashboard",
   "cell_type": "Code",
   "source": "struct Dashboard {\n    tick: u32,\n}\n\nimpl LiveView for Dashboard {\n    fn init() -> Self {\n        Self { tick: 0 }\n    }\n\n    fn tick(&mut self) -> LiveContent {\n        self.tick += 1;\n        let t = sim::read::<f64>(\"time\").unwrap_or(0.0);\n        let inertial = sim::read::<bool>(\"inertial\").unwrap_or(false);\n        let frame_name = if inertial { \"Inertial (fixed)\" } else { \"Co-rotating\" };\n\n        LiveContent::Html(format!(\n            r#\"<div style=\"font-family:monospace;background:#0d1117;color:#e6edf3;padding:16px 20px;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid #30363d;max-width:500px\">\n  <div style=\"font-size:14px;font-weight:700;color:#58a6ff;margin-bottom:12px\">Lagrange Points: CR3BP</div>\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom:8px;font-size:13px\">\n    <div style=\"display:flex;justify-content:space-between;margin-bottom:4px\">\n      <span style=\"color:#8b949e\">Mass ratio &mu;</span>\n      <span>0.012 (Earth-Moon)</span>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;justify-content:space-between;margin-bottom:4px\">\n      <span style=\"color:#8b949e\">Frame</span>\n      <span style=\"color:#58a6ff\">{frame_name}</span>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;justify-content:space-between;margin-bottom:4px\">\n      <span style=\"color:#8b949e\">Integrator</span>\n      <span>RK4</span>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;justify-content:space-between;margin-bottom:4px\">\n      <span style=\"color:#8b949e\">Sim time</span>\n      <span>{t:.2}</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"border-top:1px solid #30363d;padding-top:10px;font-size:12px\">\n    <div style=\"margin-bottom:6px;font-weight:600;color:#c9d1d9\">Objects</div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;margin-bottom:3px\"><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;background:#ff5050;margin-right:8px\"></span><span style=\"color:#ff5050\">L1</span><span style=\"color:#8b949e;margin-left:auto\">unstable: between bodies</span></div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;margin-bottom:3px\"><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;background:#ffa032;margin-right:8px\"></span><span style=\"color:#ffa032\">L2</span><span style=\"color:#8b949e;margin-left:auto\">unstable: beyond M2</span></div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;margin-bottom:3px\"><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;background:#c850ff;margin-right:8px\"></span><span style=\"color:#c850ff\">L3</span><span style=\"color:#8b949e;margin-left:auto\">unstable: beyond M1</span></div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;margin-bottom:3px\"><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;background:#50ff78;margin-right:8px\"></span><span style=\"color:#50ff78\">L4</span><span style=\"color:#8b949e;margin-left:auto\">stable: leading triangle</span></div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;margin-bottom:3px\"><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;background:#32c8ff;margin-right:8px\"></span><span style=\"color:#32c8ff\">L5</span><span style=\"color:#8b949e;margin-left:auto\">stable: trailing triangle</span></div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;margin-bottom:3px\"><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:10px;height:10px;background:#fff;transform:rotate(45deg);margin-right:8px\"></span><span style=\"color:#fff\">L2 Orbiter</span><span style=\"color:#8b949e;margin-left:auto\">Lissajous orbit (like JWST)</span></div>\n  </div>\n</div>\"#\n        ))\n    }\n\n    fn fps() -> u32 {\n        5\n    }\n}",
   "cargo_toml": null,
   "collapsed": true
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_md_cr3bp",
   "order": 4,
   "label": "The CR3BP Setup",
   "cell_type": "Markdown",
   "source": "## The Setup: Two Bodies That Matter, One That Does Not\n\nThe general three-body problem has no closed-form solution; Poincar\u00e9 settled that. The **circular restricted** three-body problem is the version that gives you real leverage: two massive primaries on circular orbits about their common barycenter, plus a third body so small it feels their gravity without perturbing them.\n\nThe standard nondimensionalization strips the problem to a single parameter. Set the separation, total mass, and orbital rate to 1; all that remains is the **mass ratio** $\\mu = M_2/(M_1+M_2)$. Earth-Moon is $\\mu \\approx 0.01215$. Sun-Earth is $\\mu \\approx 3.0 \\times 10^{-6}$. One number describes the whole system; the simulation above only needed `MU`.\n\n**The rotating frame is the load-bearing trick.** Spin your coordinates at the orbital rate and both primaries freeze: $M_1$ at $(-\\mu, 0)$, $M_2$ at $(1-\\mu, 0)$. The price is two fictitious forces. Centrifugal depends only on position, so it folds into the effective potential $\\Phi$ from the introduction. **Coriolis depends on velocity**, so it cannot be folded into any potential; that asymmetry becomes the entire story at L4 and L5.\n\nThe equations of motion in the rotating frame:\n\n$$\\ddot{x} - 2\\dot{y} = -\\frac{\\partial \\Phi}{\\partial x}, \\qquad \\ddot{y} + 2\\dot{x} = -\\frac{\\partial \\Phi}{\\partial y}$$\n\nThe $2\\dot{y}$ and $2\\dot{x}$ terms are Coriolis; they appear verbatim in the simulation's `derivs` function. The five Lagrange points are exactly the places where $\\nabla \\Phi = 0$: the forces cancel, and a particle at rest *in the rotating frame* stays at rest. L1, L2, and L3 sit on the x-axis, and solving for them means finding roots of a quintic with no closed form. So we bisect.",
   "cargo_toml": null
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_lpoint_solver",
   "order": 5,
   "label": "Solving for L1, L2, L3",
   "cell_type": "Code",
   "source": "// The collinear points solve a quintic with no closed form.\n// Bisection: 100 iterations, guaranteed convergence inside the bracket.\n\nconst MU: f64 = 0.012; // matches the simulation above\n\nfn force(x: f64) -> f64 {\n    let (x1, x2) = (-MU, 1.0 - MU);\n    let (r1, r2) = ((x - x1).abs(), (x - x2).abs());\n    x - (1.0 - MU) * (x - x1) / (r1 * r1 * r1) - MU * (x - x2) / (r2 * r2 * r2)\n}\n\nfn bisect(mut lo: f64, mut hi: f64) -> f64 {\n    let f_lo = force(lo);\n    for _ in 0..100 {\n        let mid = 0.5 * (lo + hi);\n        if force(mid) * f_lo < 0.0 { hi = mid; } else { lo = mid; }\n    }\n    0.5 * (lo + hi)\n}\n\n// Effective potential, same sign convention as the introduction.\nfn phi(x: f64, y: f64) -> f64 {\n    let r1 = ((x + MU) * (x + MU) + y * y).sqrt();\n    let r2 = ((x - 1.0 + MU) * (x - 1.0 + MU) + y * y).sqrt();\n    -(1.0 - MU) / r1 - MU / r2 - 0.5 * (x * x + y * y)\n}\n\nlet l1 = bisect(-MU + 0.01, 1.0 - MU - 0.001);\nlet l2 = bisect(1.0 - MU + 0.001, 1.5);\nlet l3 = bisect(-2.0, -MU - 0.01);\nlet (l4x, l4y) = (0.5 - MU, (3.0_f64).sqrt() / 2.0);\n\nlet rows = vec![\n    vec![\"L1\".to_string(), format!(\"{l1:.6}\"), format!(\"{:.6}\", phi(l1, 0.0))],\n    vec![\"L2\".to_string(), format!(\"{l2:.6}\"), format!(\"{:.6}\", phi(l2, 0.0))],\n    vec![\"L3\".to_string(), format!(\"{l3:.6}\"), format!(\"{:.6}\", phi(l3, 0.0))],\n    vec![\"L4/L5\".to_string(), format!(\"{l4x:.6}, y = +/-{l4y:.6}\"), format!(\"{:.6}\", phi(l4x, l4y))],\n];\n\n(\n    Table::new(vec![\"point\", \"x (rotating frame)\", \"phi\"], rows),\n    \"phi ordering: L1 < L2 < L3 < L4/L5. L1 is the lowest saddle, so it is the first gateway to open between the two realms as a particle's energy rises.\".to_string(),\n)",
   "cargo_toml": null,
   "collapsed": true
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_md_saddles",
   "order": 6,
   "label": "Saddle Points You Can Live On",
   "cell_type": "Markdown",
   "source": "## Saddle Points You Can Live On\n\nLook at the effective potential near L1, L2, or L3 and you find a **saddle**: downhill along the axis joining the primaries, uphill perpendicular to it. A marble placed there rolls away. The instability is not subtle, either: for Sun-Earth L1 and L2, a position error grows by a factor of $e$ roughly every 23 days.\n\nSo why do we put flagship observatories there? Because *unstable* is not the same as *expensive*. A saddle also supports near-periodic **halo** and **Lissajous orbits** that loop around the point without ever sitting on it. A spacecraft rides one of these and only pays to cancel the small component of drift along the unstable direction. The arithmetic is startlingly cheap: JWST budgets roughly 2-4 m/s of delta-v per year for station-keeping, with correction burns about every 21 days. For a mental model, that is a car that stays on the road with one small nudge of the wheel every three weeks.\n\nOne more directional trick: JWST can only thrust away from the Sun (turning its cold side sunward would cook the optics), so its orbit is biased toward the near side of L2 and every burn fires in the one allowed direction. Choosing the *sign* of your instability is half of mission design.\n\nThe white diamond in the simulation does a crude version of this: drift past a threshold from L2 and it cancels radial velocity and kicks back inward. Real station-keeping is a scheduled optimization problem, but the physics being fought is identical.",
   "cargo_toml": null
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_l1l2_distance",
   "order": 7,
   "label": "Sun-Earth L1/L2 Distance",
   "cell_type": "Code",
   "source": "// Sun-Earth L1 and L2 from the mass ratio alone.\n// Hill approximation: both sit near r = (mu/3)^(1/3) in units of the\n// Sun-Earth distance. Bisecting the exact equation reveals the split.\n\nconst MU: f64 = 3.003e-6; // M_earth / (M_sun + M_earth)\nconst AU_KM: f64 = 1.496e8;\n\nfn force(x: f64) -> f64 {\n    let (x1, x2) = (-MU, 1.0 - MU);\n    let (r1, r2) = ((x - x1).abs(), (x - x2).abs());\n    x - (1.0 - MU) * (x - x1) / (r1 * r1 * r1) - MU * (x - x2) / (r2 * r2 * r2)\n}\n\nfn bisect(mut lo: f64, mut hi: f64) -> f64 {\n    let f_lo = force(lo);\n    for _ in 0..200 {\n        let mid = 0.5 * (lo + hi);\n        if force(mid) * f_lo < 0.0 { hi = mid; } else { lo = mid; }\n    }\n    0.5 * (lo + hi)\n}\n\nlet x2 = 1.0 - MU;\nlet hill = (MU / 3.0).powf(1.0 / 3.0);\nlet l1 = x2 - bisect(x2 - 0.02, x2 - 1e-7);\nlet l2 = bisect(x2 + 1e-7, x2 + 0.02) - x2;\n\nlet rows = vec![\n    vec![\"Hill estimate (both)\".to_string(), format!(\"{:.0} km\", hill * AU_KM)],\n    vec![\"L1 (exact)\".to_string(), format!(\"{:.0} km\", l1 * AU_KM)],\n    vec![\"L2 (exact)\".to_string(), format!(\"{:.0} km\", l2 * AU_KM)],\n];\n\n(\n    Table::new(vec![\"distance from Earth\", \"value\"], rows),\n    \"Both land near 1.5 million km: about 4x the Moon's distance, 1% of the way to the Sun.\".to_string(),\n)",
   "cargo_toml": null,
   "collapsed": true
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_md_coriolis",
   "order": 8,
   "label": "The Coriolis Surprise",
   "cell_type": "Markdown",
   "source": "## The Coriolis Surprise at L4 and L5\n\nPlot the effective potential and L4 and L5 are **maxima**: the tops of hills, not saddles. Every instinct from rolling-marble physics says these should be the *most* unstable places in the system. And yet Jupiter's L4 and L5 hold thousands of Trojan asteroids, four billion years and counting.\n\nThe resolution is the force we could not fold into the potential. **Coriolis does the stabilizing.** A particle that starts sliding off the L4 hill picks up velocity; the Coriolis force, always at right angles to the motion, bends the trajectory sideways; the particle curls around the summit instead of leaving it. Nothing holds the particle in place. It is perpetually deflected into loops around the peak, called **tadpole orbits** after their shape.\n\nThe deflection only wins if it has time to act, and that comes down to the mass ratio. Linearize the dynamics at L4 and the motion is stable precisely when $27\\mu(1-\\mu) < 1$, the **Gascheau-Routh criterion** (Gascheau proved it in 1843). Solving the quadratic gives $\\mu_{\\text{crit}} \\approx 0.03852$, which corresponds to a primary-to-secondary mass ratio of about **24.96**. A heavier secondary than that, and the hilltop sheds particles faster than Coriolis can curl them back.\n\nAlmost every pair you care about clears the bar easily: Sun-Jupiter by about 40x, Earth-Moon by about 3.2x. The interesting failure is Pluto-Charon, at a mass ratio near 8: no stable tadpoles, no Trojans possible. The cell below does the arithmetic.",
   "cargo_toml": null
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_routh_criterion",
   "order": 9,
   "label": "The Routh Criterion, Numerically",
   "cell_type": "Code",
   "source": "// Stability boundary for L4/L5: 27 * mu * (1 - mu) = 1.\n// Solve the quadratic, then score real systems against it.\n\nlet mu_crit = 0.5 * (1.0 - (1.0_f64 - 4.0 / 27.0).sqrt());\nlet ratio_crit = (1.0 - mu_crit) / mu_crit;\n\nlet systems = [\n    (\"Sun-Jupiter\", 9.54e-4),\n    (\"Sun-Earth\", 3.00e-6),\n    (\"Earth-Moon\", 1.215e-2),\n    (\"Pluto-Charon\", 1.09e-1),\n];\n\nlet rows: Vec<Vec<String>> = systems\n    .iter()\n    .map(|&(name, mu)| {\n        let verdict = if mu < mu_crit { \"stable\" } else { \"UNSTABLE\" };\n        vec![\n            name.to_string(),\n            format!(\"{mu:.2e}\"),\n            verdict.to_string(),\n            format!(\"{:.1}x\", mu_crit / mu),\n        ]\n    })\n    .collect();\n\n(\n    Table::new(vec![\"system\", \"mu\", \"L4/L5\", \"margin to the line\"], rows),\n    format!(\"mu_crit = {mu_crit:.5}, i.e. L4/L5 are stable when M1/M2 > {ratio_crit:.2}\"),\n)",
   "cargo_toml": null,
   "collapsed": true
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_5",
   "order": 10,
   "label": "Real-World Applications",
   "cell_type": "Markdown",
   "source": "## Real-World Applications\n\nThere is hardware parked at most of these points right now. A quick catalog.\n\n### L1: Solar Observation\n\nSun-Earth L1 sits 1.5 million km sunward, and its value is simple: the Sun never sets there. NASA/ESA's **SOHO** has watched the Sun from an L1 halo orbit continuously since 1996. **DSCOVR** and **ACE** sit there as upstream sentries: solar wind reaches L1 roughly 30-60 minutes before Earth, enough warning to safe a power grid.\n\n### L2: Deep Space Telescopes\n\nSun-Earth L2 is the same distance in the opposite direction, prime real estate for a different reason: the Sun, Earth, and Moon all crowd into one small patch of sky. The **James Webb Space Telescope** hides all three behind a single sunshield and lets its optics passively cool to about 40 K, which is the whole game for infrared astronomy. **Gaia**, **Euclid**, and **Planck** chose it for the same thermal quiet. The white diamond in the simulation shows this kind of **Lissajous orbit**.\n\n### L3: Science Fiction Favorite\n\nThe Sun-Earth L3 point (on the far side of the Sun) is a staple of sci-fi as the hiding place for a \"Counter-Earth.\" In reality it is the least useful point of the five: unstable, permanently out of direct radio contact, and further perturbed by Venus and Jupiter. Nothing has ever been stationed there.\n\n### L4 & L5: Trojan Asteroids\n\nJupiter's L4 and L5 points harbor over **12,000 known Trojan asteroids**. NASA's **Lucy** mission is touring several of them because they are dynamically pristine, parked undisturbed since the solar system formed. The Sun-Earth L4/L5 regions have also been proposed as sites for future space colonies (the **O'Neill cylinder** concept).\n\n### Co-Rotating vs Inertial Frames\n\nIn the **co-rotating frame**, the primaries and L-points are fixed; you see the dynamics relative to the system. In the **inertial frame**, everything orbits the barycenter; you see the true paths through space. Switch between them using the slider to build intuition for both perspectives!\n\n### Station-Keeping\n\nThe collinear points (L1, L2, L3) are all **unstable**; spacecraft must perform periodic thruster burns to remain nearby. They typically follow **Lissajous** or **halo orbits** around the Lagrange point rather than sitting exactly on it.\n\nThe triangular points (L4, L5) are **linearly stable** when the mass ratio satisfies $\\mu < \\mu_{\\text{crit}} \\approx 0.0385$ (the Gascheau/Routh criterion). This covers all planet-Sun pairs in our solar system.",
   "cargo_toml": null
  }
 ],
 "description": "Live simulation of test particles near all five Lagrange points with frame selection and L2 Lissajous orbiter.",
